<Profile title="Connecting data collection and reporting — Dave Bettner" description="A Workiva implementation spanning bidirectional API integration, SSO, an audit trail, and user acceptance testing." page="experience">
<section class="page-intro wrap"><h1>Connecting data collection and reporting.</h1><p class="lead">A Workiva implementation I led at Citrin Cooperman, from integration through user acceptance testing, go-live, and executive sign-off.</p><p class="story-context">Manager of Digital Services, Workiva · December 2024–October 2025</p></section>
<section class="paper"><div class="story-layout wrap">
<aside class="story-brief"><h2>The scope</h2><p>Connect the customer’s system of record with a data-collection and reporting workflow in Workiva.</p><p>The implementation included a bidirectional API integration, single sign-on, and an audit trail.</p><p class="disclosure">Client identity and implementation materials are not public. This is an engagement summary, not a live client demonstration.</p></aside>
<div class="story-body"><section><h2>My responsibility</h2><p>I led the implementation and user acceptance testing through go-live and executive sign-off. In my role at Citrin Cooperman, I also managed a five-person Workiva implementation team, coached consultants, resolved customer escalations, and led testing and training.</p><p>This was delivery leadership across the technical work and the people using it—not simply configuration of a reporting tool.</p></section>
<section><h2>What had to come together</h2><dl class="scope-definitions"><div><dt>Bidirectional integration</dt><dd>Connecting Workiva and the customer’s system of record through an API.</dd></div><div><dt>Access and traceability</dt><dd>Single sign-on and an audit trail within the implementation scope.</dd></div><div><dt>Acceptance and launch</dt><dd>User acceptance testing, go-live, and executive sign-off.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section><h2>The delivery lesson</h2><p>For me, an integration is not the whole implementation. It also needs a customer who understands the workflow, a team prepared to support it, and a clear acceptance process.</p><p>That is the connection between my technical and consulting work: making the system usable, explaining it to the people responsible for it, and carrying the engagement through launch.</p></section>
